## Why This Is Asked

Assess practical low-level memory management skills, lock-free primitives, and performance considerations for multithreaded C/C++ code.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size allocators
- Lock-free free-list with CAS
- Thread-local caches
- Alignment and fragmentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for init, malloc, and free
typedef struct Node { void* next; } Node;
static void* head;

void init_arena(void* arena_base, size_t size) {
  // split arena into 32-byte blocks and link on free-list
}

void* my_malloc() {
  void* old = atomic_load(&head);
  void* next = ((Node*)old)->next;
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &old, next)) return old;
  // retry loop omitted for brevity
}

void my_free(void* p) {
  Node* node = (Node*)p;
  void* old;
  do {
    old = atomic_load(&head);
    node->next = old;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &old, node));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization, atomic primitives, and memory ordering in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- atomic_flag basics
- memory_order_acquire/release semantics
- backoff to avoid livelock
- cache-line padding to prevent false sharing

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>

typedef struct { 
    alignas(64) atomic_flag flag; 
} spinlock_t;

void spinlock_init(spinlock_t* s) {
    atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag);
}

void spinlock_lock(spinlock_t* s) {
    while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
        // Simple backoff to prevent livelock
        for (volatile int i = 0; i < 100; i++);
    }
}

void spinlock_unlock(spinlock_t* s) {
    atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release);
}

bool spinlock_trylock(spinlock_t* s) {
    return !atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire);
}
```

## Stress Test
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

spinlock_t lock;
long long counter = 0;

void* thread_func(void* arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
        spinlock_lock(&lock);
        counter++;
        spinlock_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    spinlock_init(&lock);
    pthread_t t1, t2;

    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, thread_func, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, thread_func, NULL);

    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);

    printf("Final counter: %lld\n", counter);
    return 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of low level synchronization, memory ordering, and reusability. A barrier is fundamental in frame sync, parallel tasks, and NUMA awareness, but many candidates overfit to simple mutex patterns.

## Key Concepts

- C11 atomics and memory ordering
- Sense-reversing barrier design
- Reuse safety and race avoidance
- Spin-wait costs and pause hints
- Microbenchmark methodology

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <sched.h>
volatile atomic_int arrivals;
volatile atomic_int generation;

void barrier(int N){
  int gen = atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire);
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&arrivals, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) == N-1){
    atomic_store_explicit(&arrivals, 0, memory_order_release);
    atomic_store_explicit(&generation, 1 - gen, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire) == gen){
      sched_yield();
    }
  }
}
```

